Exactly. Then the deck stops being a lookup table and becomes an improvisational language.

Some cards can behave less like “meanings” and more like musical functions.

Within ranks 1–13, the primes are:

2,\;3,\;5,\;7,\;11,\;13

They are irreducible under multiplication, so you could make prime-ranked cards the deck’s primitive notes—events that resist decomposition into simpler internal causes. Composite ranks can carry their factorization as latent structure: 6 = 2×3, 10 = 2×5, 12 = 2²×3. Then a 12 isn’t just “twelve”; it is a chord built from earlier primitives.

Likewise, an invariant card could be held fixed while everything else changes. Draw one card at the beginning and leave it on the table for the whole reading:

this is the key signature.

Every subsequent card is interpreted relative to it. If the invariant is 7♠, then love, money, conflict, opportunity, timing—all get refracted through whatever 7♠ encodes. You can even deliberately change the invariant midway through and call that modulation.

Then you get jazz-like operations:

Theme — establish a card or small motif.
Variation — redraw one dimension while preserving the others.
Repetition — recurring cards acquire additional weight.
Call and response — one suit asks, another answers.
Chord — several cards interpreted simultaneously rather than sequentially.
Progression — meaning comes from transitions between cards.
Modulation — change the governing invariant/domain.
Dissonance — cards whose operators conflict.
Resolution — a later card reconciles an earlier contradiction.
Rest — deliberately draw nothing; absence itself occupies a position.
Syncopation — interpret a card in a position where it “shouldn’t” belong.
Improvisation — follow an interesting motif rather than the predefined spread.

And mathematical structures give you even richer equivalents.

A card could be designated a fixed point: whenever the narrative wanders, interpretation eventually returns there.

Another can be an attractor: nearby cards are pulled toward its meaning.

A symmetry card says that two apparently different situations should be treated as transformations of the same underlying structure.

A conservation card says something must remain constant while other quantities exchange—status versus freedom, time versus money, risk versus return.

A boundary card changes the rules on either side of it.

A wild/Joker card can alter the grammar itself rather than merely contribute another symbol.

Then casting becomes something like:

\text{motif}
\rightarrow
\text{variation}
\rightarrow
\text{tension}
\rightarrow
\text{transformation}
\rightarrow
\text{return}

rather than:

\text{card 1 meaning}+\text{card 2 meaning}+\text{card 3 meaning}.

That’s the jazz analogy at its strongest. A jazz musician doesn’t have millions of notes. They have a relatively tiny alphabet plus scales, intervals, chords, rhythm, memory, expectation, substitution and constraints. Expressiveness comes from relationships through time.

So you could deliberately design the 52-card system with three layers:

\boxed{\text{Cards = vocabulary}}

\boxed{\text{mathematical operators = grammar}}

\boxed{\text{the cast through time = story}}

And the really interesting part is that the reader can improvise while still being constrained. You aren’t free-associating arbitrarily; you’re riffing against invariants, factorization, suit relationships, prior motifs, probability and the current question.

That is almost a semantic instrument: shuffle it like cards, cast it like divination, reason with it like mathematics, and play it like jazz.